# executor/requirements.in
# Minimal dependencies of the Atlas executor (standalone client, manual
# installation via the CLI). Edit HERE; executor/requirements.txt is generated
# from this, locked and with each file's hash (python scripts/travar_python.py).
# That is what gets installed — `pip install -r executor/requirements.txt`,
# preferably in a venv; pip checks the hashes on its own.
#
# These used to be floors (>=), and each machine downloaded the newest version
# of everything at install time: no review and no quarantine, the entry point
# for a freshly published malicious version. Now the versions come from the
# full lock: the script compiles this file with
# `--constraint executor/requirements-full.txt` (on the command line, and not
# via a `-c` here, because of Dependabot — see PARES in
# scripts/travar_python.py). They are the same as Docker's and the desktop
# app's, and only change through a PR.

# WebSocket client
websockets

# Async HTTP client (authentication + key registration)
httpx

# CA certificates (fixes SSL on macOS where Python doesn't use the system keychain)
certifi

# Criptografia (X25519, Ed25519, AES-256-GCM, HKDF)
cryptography

# Environment variables
python-dotenv

# System metrics (CPU, RAM, disk)
psutil

# Live dashboard in the terminal. Optional in practice: without it the executor
# starts normally and keeps the line-by-line log (see EXECUTOR_DASHBOARD in the README).
rich

# Flow engine (installed via pip install -e . at the repo root)
# or via PYTHONPATH pointing to the root
OWSLib
